                    briefing_title_r1:      `Round 1 — Diagnose the learner segments`,
                    briefing_text_r1:       `Before any content, decide <strong>who this program is for</strong>. Three segments want very different products: price-sensitive <em>mid-managers</em> ($1,800–2,400, high non-completion risk), employer-paid <em>corporate cohorts</em> ($3,500–4,500 but only ~40 feasible seats this summer), and outcome-driven <em>career-changers</em> ($2,800–3,200 for a credential with a job result). Segment choice is the upstream decision that constrains format, price and content. The trap: building a “course for everyone” that fits no one's job-to-be-done. And anchor your eventual price to the segment's willingness-to-pay, not to your build cost.`,
                    briefing_title_r2:      `Round 2 — Choose the format within the $180k cap`,
                    briefing_text_r2:       `Pick the delivery format for your segment and build a line-item development budget that stays under <strong>$180,000 (including a marketing reserve)</strong>. Self-paced is cheapest (~$90k) but only ~45% complete; live cohort (~$150k) completes ~70% with strong NPS; in-person (~$175k + per-cohort cost) completes ~85% but <em>breaches the cap once you fund acquisition</em>. The cheapest format to build is rarely the one your segment values or finishes. Fund the build your chosen format actually needs, and keep a real marketing reserve.`,
                    briefing_title_r3:      `Round 3 — Price the offering and prove break-even`,
                    briefing_text_r3:       `Set the learner price and build the break-even model: <strong>contribution margin = price − variable cost</strong>, and <strong>break-even seats = $180,000 ÷ contribution margin</strong>. You need 60 paid seats across two cohorts. Raising price protects margin but can push required enrollment <em>above what the segment can supply</em>; a low price may never recover the build. Anchor to your segment's willingness-to-pay, and consider a corporate tier to backfill a thin public funnel.`,
                    briefing_title_r4:      `Round 4 — Pitch the program to the Dean's gate`,
                    briefing_text_r4:       `Present the offering as one coherent design: the segment it serves, why this format fits them, the price and the break-even logic, and the completion/NPS you expect. The Dean will press: <em>“Why not just build it self-paced and pocket the margin?”</em> Defend it in <strong>renewal-revenue</strong> terms, put a real scorecard in front of the finance reviewer, and name your contingency if the first cohort opens at 22 against a 60-seat break-even.`,

                    r1_segment_label:       `Which learner segment is this program's primary design target?`,
                    r1_wtp_label:           `Anchor the price to the segment's willingness-to-pay (WTP) or to your build cost?`,
                    r1_rigor_label:         `How rigorously does your team size the addressable demand before committing? (%)`,
                    r1_rigor_readout:       `Demand-sizing rigor (can this segment actually supply 60 seats?):`,
                    seg_changer:            `Career-changers / upskillers — outcome-driven, will pay $2,800–3,200 for a credential with a job result`,
                    seg_corporate:          `Corporate cohorts — employer-paid $3,500–4,500/seat, but only ~40 feasible seats this summer`,
                    seg_midmgr:             `Mid-managers — large but price-sensitive ($1,800–2,400), high non-completion risk`,
