                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_changer:       `<strong>Career-changers are a strong primary segment.</strong> They are outcome-driven, will pay $2,800–3,200 for a credential with a job result, and complete when supported — so they feed the renewal engine. Now fit the format and price to that learner's job-to-be-done.`,
                    coach_r1_corporate:     `<strong>Corporate cohorts pay the most per seat</strong> — $3,500–4,500 — but only ~40 feasible seats exist across two cohorts this summer, and break-even needs 60. Lovely margin, hard to fill alone. You will likely need a tier or a second segment to reach 60.`,
                    coach_r1_midmgr:        `<strong>Mid-managers are large but price-sensitive.</strong> At $1,800–2,400 your contribution margin is thin and you need many more seats to break even — and their high non-completion risk drags the renewal engine. A defensible choice only if the format fits their time constraint.`,
                    coach_r1_everyone:      `<strong>You designed a course for everyone — so for no one.</strong> Mid-managers want it self-paced at $2,000; corporate buyers want customization at $4,000. Those are different products. Which learner did you build for, and what does the other segment do when your design doesn't fit them?`,
                    coach_r1_segfirst:      `<strong>Segment-first, value-anchored — exactly right.</strong> You chose a coherent learner (career-changers) and decided to price to their outcome, not your costs. That single choice now lets format, price and content line up instead of fighting each other.`,
                    coach_r1_costplus:      `<strong>You're pricing to your costs, not your learner.</strong> Cost-plus marking up the build leaves money on the table with a segment that will happily pay for an outcome — and then you need far more enrollments to break even. Why price to your costs instead of your learner's willingness-to-pay?`,
                    coach_r1_norigor:       `<strong>You committed without sizing the demand.</strong> Before you fall in love with a price, ask whether the segment can actually supply 60 paid seats across two cohorts. Skipping that is how teams design a program that is profitable per seat and impossible to fill.`,
                    coach_r2_live:          `<strong>Live cohort fits an outcome-driven segment.</strong> ~70% completion and strong NPS feed the renewal engine, and at ~$150k it sits under the cap with room for marketing. This is the format most of your candidate segments actually value.`,
                    coach_r2_selfpaced:     `<strong>Self-paced is cheap to build and scales beautifully — and ~55% of learners quit before finishing.</strong> Those drop-outs don't renew, and the employer who sponsored them won't buy a second cohort. You saved ~$60k on development. What did the lost renewals and dead NPS cost you?`,
                    coach_r2_inperson:      `<strong>In-person gives you ~85% completion and the best NPS — and a ~$175k build before you've spent a dollar on marketing.</strong> Your cap is $180k. You're already over once you fund acquisition. Either the format changes or the program doesn't launch.`,
                    coach_r2_underfund:     `<strong>You under-funded the build this format needs.</strong> A thin curriculum/media/SME budget for the format you chose means a weaker program your segment will feel — lower completion and NPS. Fund the build the format actually requires.`,
                    coach_r2_nomarketing:   `<strong>No real marketing reserve, no funnel.</strong> A great design nobody hears about never reaches 60 paid seats. The cap is meant to be inclusive of acquisition — protect a reserve to fill the cohorts.`,
                    coach_r2_overcap:       `<strong>You're over the $180k cap.</strong> The Dean's guardrail is binding and inclusive of marketing — a program that breaches it doesn't launch, the build is written off, and you lose budget standing. Either the format changes or the line items come down.`,
                    coach_r3_breakeven:     `<strong>Break-even at or under 60 seats — that's the discipline.</strong> Your contribution margin recovers the $180k build within the mandated 60 paid enrollments. Now make sure the price is one your segment will actually pay and the funnel can fill.`,
                    coach_r3_tight:         `<strong>Break-even is above 60 but reachable.</strong> Your margin recovers the build, but only if the funnel over-performs. Tighten variable cost or lift price toward the segment's WTP to bring required enrollment down to 60.`,
                    coach_r3_unrecoverable: `<strong>This price never recovers the build.</strong> With this contribution margin you need far more than 60 seats — more than the segment can supply. A low price fills seats but leaves the $180k development cost unrecovered.`,
                    coach_r3_unfillable:    `<strong>Profitable per seat, impossible to fill.</strong> You priced well above the segment's willingness-to-pay to protect margin — but the addressable segment can't supply the seats at that price. Where do the missing enrollments come from?`,
                    coach_r3_underpriced:   `<strong>You left money on the table.</strong> Pricing far below the segment's willingness-to-pay thins your margin and forces you to fill many more seats. Anchor the price to the learner's outcome, not the floor.`,
                    coach_r3_clears:        `<strong>This clears break-even at a fillable price.</strong> Margin recovers the build within 60 seats AND the segment can supply them at this price. That is pricing for services done right — value to the learner, recoverability for the unit.`,
                    coach_r3_short:         `<strong>Projected enrollments are below the 60-seat break-even.</strong> Either the price is unfillable for this segment, the funnel is too thin, or you need a corporate tier to backfill. Reconcile price, segment supply and the funnel.`,
                    coach_r4_renewal:       `<strong>That's the renewal-revenue defense.</strong> Self-paced's cheap build is a false economy — its ~45% completion kills the repeat enrollment and employer renewals that drive ~40% of new revenue. You defended the design on the economics, not on taste.`,
                    coach_r4_margin:        `<strong>“Higher per-seat margin” is the trap.</strong> Self-paced saves build cost and scales — and abandons more than half your learners. Those drop-outs don't renew. Per-seat margin isn't the unit's economics; renewal is.`,
                    coach_r4_prestige:      `<strong>“We only do premium” isn't an argument the Dean accepts.</strong> Defend the format on completion, NPS and renewal revenue — the economics — not on institutional taste.`,
                    coach_r4_runanyway:     `<strong>Running under-filled misses break-even this cycle.</strong> It's honest, but the build gets written off against the unit's margin and you lose budget standing. A backfill tier would have protected the launch.`,
                    coach_r4_nocontingency: `<strong>You're pitching with no fallback.</strong> Your plan assumes 60 paid enrollments and the first cohort opens at 22. Run it under-filled, delay the second cohort, or open a lower-priced tier to backfill? Presenting to the Dean with no contingency is how programs get killed at the gate.`,
                    coach_r4_revenueonly:   `<strong>A revenue-only scorecard hides the risk.</strong> The finance reviewer needs enrollment vs break-even, completion and NPS too — a net-revenue number alone can mask an under-enrolled, low-completion design that won't renew.`,
                    coach_r4_boardready:    `<strong>That's a gate-ready pitch.</strong> A renewal-anchored defense, a full scorecard, and a named backfill contingency for under-enrollment — you presented a coherent design that fits a segment, clears break-even, and survives a soft first cohort.`,

                    sum_default:            `Decision made.`,
