                    insight_approved:       `Approved and self-sustaining: you designed a coherent offering — a defined segment, a format that fits them, a value-anchored price that clears the 60-seat break-even under the $180k cap, and a completion/NPS profile that feeds the renewal engine. This is what professional-education program design looks like done right.`,
                    insight_fragile:        `Approved but fragile: you cleared break-even, but thin design fit or a weak renewal profile means the program may not feed repeat enrollment. The Dean approved it; the next development cycle depends on whether it renews.`,
                    insight_overcap:        `Over the cap — not launched: the most ambitious design breached the $180,000 guardrail before marketing was even funded. The build is written off and you lose budget standing. The best pedagogy is not free; the cap forces design discipline.`,
                    insight_short:          `Under-enrolled: projected paid seats fell short of the 60-seat break-even. A program that mis-targets its segment — right content, wrong buyer — or prices itself unfillably fails as surely as one that is badly built.`,
                    insight_renewal:        `The renewal engine is weak: low completion and NPS mean drop-outs who don't return and employers who won't buy a second cohort. In professional education, completion is economics, not a vanity metric.`,
                    insight_margin:         `Margin health is thin: your contribution margin barely recovers the build, leaving the program exposed if a cohort opens soft. Price to the segment's willingness-to-pay and control variable cost.`,
                    insight_breakeven:      `Break-even sits above 60 seats: at this contribution margin you need more enrollments than the mandate — and possibly more than the segment can supply. Lift price toward WTP or cut variable cost.`,
                    insight_lasts:          `This is a design that lasts: fit, margin and the renewal engine moving together, with break-even cleared at a fillable price. The unit gets repeat enrollment and budget standing for the next cycle.`,

                    hint_overcap:           `You're over the $180k cap — the program can't launch as designed. Drop to a format and line-item budget that fits, with a marketing reserve still inside the cap.`,
                    hint_breakeven:         `Break-even is far above 60 seats. Lift the price toward your segment's willingness-to-pay or cut variable cost so the build is recoverable within the mandate.`,
                    hint_renewal:           `The renewal engine is collapsing — completion and NPS are too low. Reconsider the self-paced format; a higher-completion format feeds repeat enrollment that outweighs the cheaper build.`,
                    hint_enroll:            `Projected enrollments are below 60. Reconcile price against your segment's supply, push the funnel, or open a corporate tier to backfill.`,
                    hint_strong:            `Strong design — fit, renewal and enrollment all healthy. Lock it in with a renewal-anchored pitch, a full scorecard and a named under-enrollment contingency.`,

